.sp
.in 8n
Shows the regular file $PREFIX/lib/usilic/HELP.en.epub (\*[usilic-i]~/.local/lib/usilic/\*[usilic-ie]HELP.en.epub etc.) as an \fBEPUB\fR. This \fBmode\fR shows text laid out according to the CSS bundled in HELP.en.epub, so on a screen started with the -c option, changing the line spacing, the kind and size of the font etc. cannot be used. On Windows, which starts only in GUI, this is no problem; elsewhere, restart as needed with ^Og (the CUI / GUI toggle key) or the -g option.
.br
.sp
^G (or a right click of the mouse) in the text moves to the list of chapters. This is the contents file following \*[usilic-i]nav.xhtml\*[usilic-ie] in the .epub format (required by the EPUB3 specification); for text without contents such as HELP.en.epub, \*[usilic-i]usilic(1)\*[usilic-ie] treats lines ending with an added tab code as chapter titles, so typing ^O^J^S^Q^I$^M (show the list of lines ending with a tab code searched with \fBEGREP\fR) gives the same list. By the way, ^Jp^Q^I$^M (split the \fBHELP\fR screen with \fBPART\fR) also shows a list that looks the same, but a manual is easier to follow when read straight through, so \fBHELP\fR does not use it. Moving to the previous / next chapter with the ; / : keys works the same, though, in any \fBEPUB\fR including HELP.en.epub.
.br
.sp
^Os on the \fBHELP\fR screen switches the scroll \fBmode\fR between continuous and per chapter (only one chapter on the screen). 
.br
^[[ / ^[] change \fILINE-HEIGHT\fR, and ^[{ / ^[} change \fILETTER-SPACING\fR, to increase / decrease the line spacing and the letter spacing (the width of one half-width character) respectively (twice that for Japanese). The setting is kept for the next start.
.br
.sp
^Ot switches between serif and sans-serif. What becomes serif is, in \fBEPUB\fR, the characters the CSS specifies as serif, and on other screens, full-width characters (Japanese); half-width characters stay in the monospaced font. The setting is kept for the next start.
.br
.sp
^J^F shows the list of \fBFONT\fR (monospaced) and \fISANS-SERIF\fR / \fISERIF\fR (with Japanese) fonts found by fc-list (the current font is underlined). When started from a terminal, \fBFONT\fR begins with "terminal font"; choosing it empties \fIFONT\fR and goes back to the terminal's font. Choosing with ^M or a left click switches at once and writes it to the \fIFONT\fR setting.
.br
.sp
In HELP.en.epub, the blue numbers (such as [1]) after the names of the setting items in \*[usilic-i]usilicrc\*[usilic-ie] are mainly links to notes; ^M (or a click) jumps to one, and ^G (or a right click of the mouse) returns to the original place\*[usilic-note].
.br
.sp
^T^H opens \fBHELP\fR in another window as a child-process \*[usilic-i]usilic(1)\*[usilic-ie] (same as -H). Operations in the child \*[usilic-i]usilic(1)\*[usilic-ie] are not kept in the history, so the child's history lists are almost empty.
.in 0
.sp
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
\fBKEY\fR          ：key assignments\*[usilic-tab]
.in 0
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
           ^K：show the list of keys
.in 0
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
         ^J^K：start \fBKEY\fR from a prompt
.in 0
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
         ^T^K：open \fBKEY\fR in a child window (same as -K)
.in 0
.sp
.in 8n
It looks like \fBHELP\fR after ^O^S : ^M (\fBFGREP\fR with a full-width colon), but ^M (or a left click) on the highlighted area runs the function shown, and the h key jumps to the place in \fBHELP\fR that explains it.
.br
.sp
^T^K opens \fBKEY\fR in another window as a child-process \*[usilic-i]usilic(1)\*[usilic-ie] (same as -K). A \*[usilic-i]usilic(1)\*[usilic-ie] started this way as a pager (next chapter) shows "\fBUSILIC\fR" instead of "\fBusilic-1.5b\fR" at the bottom right, and "(parent's PID)" is added at the end of the first line (the command line) in \fBINFO\fR (to tell it from the original \*[usilic-i]usilic(1)\*[usilic-ie]).
.in 0
.sp
.in 19n
.ti 0n
Viewing files    ：pager\*[usilic-tab]
.in 0
.sp
.in 8n
When started with file arguments ("usilic file"^M) or through standard input without arguments ("usilic < file"^M etc.), \*[usilic-i]usilic(1)\*[usilic-ie] works as a pager for viewing files. When started with several file arguments, n/p move to the next/previous file, x/f to the first/last file, and q quits. After startup, \*[usilic-i]usilic(1)\*[usilic-ie] shows a status line like the one below (mode-line below) on the bottom line of the screen.
.in 0
